# DESK 4: subject, reach and direction are different premises, not three citations.
item('metaphysics-objection','Philosophy already studies being—even God. Why add another teaching?','The student argues that a science covering all being leaves no subject for another teaching.',D,'Attributed objection — Aquinas, Summa I q.1 a.1 objection 2.')
item('salvation-end','Our final end exceeds what reason can discover, Aquinas argues','Aquinas places human fulfilment beyond unaided reason’s discovery.',D,'Attributed premise — Aquinas, Summa I q.1 a.1; this end is part of his theological account.')
item('known-destination','We must know our destination to direct our lives','Choosing a course requires some knowledge of the end being sought.',D,'Attributed premise — Aquinas, Summa I q.1 a.1.')
item('reason-limits','Studying God does not mean discovering every human end','The objection confuses having a subject with exhausting everything that can be known about it.',D,'Reconstructed reply — Aquinas, Summa I q.1 a.1, especially reply 2.')
item('philosophical-inquiry','A philosophy of God must still show our destination','Naming philosophy’s subject has not yet shown how people can direct their lives.',D,'Editorial partial argument from Aquinas, Summa I q.1 a.1; this leaves the question of reason’s reach open.')
item('reason-and-revelation','An undiscoverable destination would have to be disclosed','If this end exceeds discovery but must be known, unaided inquiry is insufficient.',D,'Reconstructed inference — Aquinas, Summa I q.1 a.1; conditional on the stated theological end.')
item('reason-serving-theology','Revelation can add knowledge without making philosophy redundant','A shared subject does not entail equal means or reach. Aquinas therefore gives revealed teaching a task alongside reasoning.',D,'Reconstructed answer to the student — Aquinas, Summa I q.1 a.1, reply 2.')
item('nature-perfected','“Grace does not destroy nature but perfects it.”','Augustine’s inward aid does not replace the person who acts; Aquinas’s revealed teaching need not replace the person who reasons. The comparison concerns assisted activity, not identical doctrines.',D+['aug-grace'],'Short quotation and editorial comparison — Aquinas, Summa I q.1 a.8 reply 2, English Dominican translation; Augustine, On the Grace of Christ I.3, 6–7.')
item('fire-water','Fire destroys water precisely through its effective natural power','Aquinas’s physical example makes corruption follow from a thing acting effectively, not only from an agent being defective.',E,'Attributed example — Aquinas, Summa I q.49 a.1. This uses his natural philosophy, not modern chemistry.')
item('aquinas-tree','The good tree names a good will, Aquinas argues','Aquinas follows Augustine in reading the tree morally rather than as a rule assigning everything harmful to a separate creator.',E,'Attributed interpretation — Aquinas, Summa I q.49 a.1 reply 1; explicitly cites Augustine.')
item('aquinas-evil-answer','A harmful effect need not come from an evil nature','Against the demand for an opposed source, the fire example distinguishes a power’s own perfection from the loss it causes elsewhere.',E+['two-principles'],'Editorial comparison of arguments — Aquinas, Summa I q.49 a.1 and Book of Two Principles I.1–2. It does not establish that Aquinas read this treatise.')
item('aquinas-tree-answer','The tree dispute turns on wills versus origins','Aquinas’s reading blocks the move from the verse to opposed creators; the Italian writer applies the image to origins instead.',E+['two-principles','matthew-tree'],'Editorial comparison — Aquinas, Summa I q.49 a.1 reply 1 and Book of Two Principles I.1–2. Distinct readings, not a verdict on Scripture.')
# DESK 5: the ritual is a separate witness, not a metaphysical stamp.
item('ritual-leaf','John baptizes with water; Christ baptizes with the Spirit','The rite invokes the water/Spirit contrast to explain what kind of baptism it claims.',R,'Attributed paraphrase — Lyon PA 36 ritual IV.8–11, Harris critical edition.')
item('gospel-and-hands','The elder lays book and hands upon the candidate','The rubric directs a physical ceremony with a Gospel book and human hands.',R,'Attributed paraphrase — Lyon PA 36 ritual IV.49–52 and VI.36; Harris critical edition.')
item('acts-hands','Peter and John lay hands; the Samaritans receive the Spirit','The ritual cites Acts as a precedent for receiving the Spirit through laying on hands.',R,'Attributed paraphrase — Lyon PA 36 ritual IV.15–18; Harris critical edition.')
item('ritual-promise','The candidate promises not to lie, swear or kill','The candidate undertakes a continuing discipline, including concrete restraints on conduct.',R,'Attributed paraphrase — Lyon PA 36 ritual IV.31–32, 42–47; Harris critical edition. Not an exhaustive list of the undertaking.')
item('spiritual-baptism','Lyon’s laying-on of hands claims the apostles’ spiritual baptism','The ritual connects its own gesture with the apostolic precedent. The claimed continuity is part of its account, not independently demonstrated transmission.',R,'Reconstructed argument — Lyon PA 36 ritual IV.3, 12–18, 49–52.')
item('embodied-spiritual-rite','Lyon’s spiritual baptism is not a bodiless event','The contrast with water does not eliminate material action: hands and a book remain part of the ceremony.',R,'Inference from the ritual’s claim and rubric — Lyon PA 36 IV.8–18, 49–52.')
item('ritual-origins-limit','Book and hands do not decide between rival creators','The rite’s bodily practice neither proves Augustine’s account of matter nor establishes the Italian writer’s opposed origins. Its immediate claim concerns receiving the Spirit.',R+['aug-evil','two-principles'],'Editorial comparison with the earlier origins dispute. Lyon PA 36 is a separate witness from the Book of Two Principles.')
item('ritual-discipline','Receiving the Spirit begins a pledged way of life','The candidate’s undertaking links reception to continuing conduct, rather than a single bodily gesture detached from later life.',R,'Reconstructed relation — Lyon PA 36 ritual IV.3, 31–32, 42–52. A description of this rite, not all medieval dissenters.')
# DESK 6: three distinct causal premises; tools and appearances keep their strengths.
item('water-and-word','Baptism washes the body but is said to cleanse the heart','Aquinas cites Augustine’s baptismal claim: an outward rite is credited with an inward effect.',G,'Attributed paraphrase — Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1, on the contrary. The efficacy is a theological claim, not an observation.')
item('god-gives-grace','Only God gives grace by his own power','For Aquinas, God alone is the principal cause of grace; this is stronger than saying a minister possesses it.',G,'Attributed premise — Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1.')
item('saw','A carpenter’s saw cuts but cannot design a chest','The tool has a real cutting action without possessing the craftsperson’s art.',G,'Editorial adaptation of Aquinas’s tool/maker example — Summa III q.62 a.1 uses an axe and a couch.')
item('craftsperson','The craftsperson directs the tool toward the intended work','The maker supplies the design and directs the tool’s action.',G,'Editorial adaptation of the instrument analogy — Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1.')
item('instrument-analogy','The tool truly acts, under the maker’s direction','Its contribution is real but dependent; tool and maker need not be competing explanations of the same work.',G,'Reconstructed analogy — Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1. It illustrates agency, not evidence that baptism works.')
item('sacramental-difficulty','How can bodily baptism confer what only God gives?','The baptismal claim appears to assign an inward divine effect to a created action.',G,'Reconstructed problem — Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1 objections 2–3 and on the contrary.')
item('borrowed-cleansing','Baptism could act by another’s power, like a tool','Instrumental action offers a possible way to understand the claimed inward effect without attributing it to water’s independent nature.',G,'Reconstructed partial analogy — Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1; the analogy does not prove efficacy.')
item('dependent-instrument','An instrument need not rival the principal giver','A dependent instrument’s action leaves the principal agent the source of the power used.',G,'Reconstructed causal distinction — Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1.')
item('instrument-of-grace','Aquinas: baptism gives grace as God’s instrument, not a rival','Aquinas treats baptism as a real instrumental cause under God’s principal agency, not merely a token that accompanies a separate gift.',G,'Attributed argument — Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1. Reconstructing it does not establish the sacrament’s efficacy.')
item('appearance-notes','After consecration, the familiar colour and taste remain','The sensory report registers continuing appearances rather than an observable replacement.',U,'Observation as framed by Aquinas — Summa III q.75 aa.2, 5.')
item('eucharist-leaf','This is Christ’s body, not bread, Aquinas maintains','Aquinas denies that the substance of bread remains after consecration.',U,'Attributed theological claim — Aquinas, Summa III q.75 aa.2, 4.')
item('substance-and-appearance','Aquinas’s asserted change concerns substance, not the remaining appearances','The distinction reconciles the report with his claim by assigning them different objects. The sensory report alone cannot establish that substantial change.',U,'Reconstructed distinction — Aquinas, Summa III q.75 aa.2, 4–5.')
item('baptism-media','Lyon lays hands; Aquinas washes—both claim an inward gift','The accounts attach reception to different bodily rites, not to an absence of bodily action.',R+G,'Editorial comparison — Lyon PA 36 IV.3, 8–18; Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1.')
item('sacramental-disagreement','Lyon’s prayer and hands differ from Aquinas’s instrumental water','Lyon claims reception of the Spirit through prayer and laying on hands, appealing to apostolic precedent. Aquinas explains baptismal water as a dependent efficient cause. The ritual does not supply an equivalent causal taxonomy; it is not being classified as a mere-symbol theory.',R+G,'Editorial comparison — Lyon PA 36 IV.3, 8–18; Aquinas, Summa III q.62 a.1. Different rites and explanatory forms, not a manufactured symbol-versus-cause opposition.')
# DESK 8: assistance, justification and purpose of works are not interchangeable.
item('promise-of-christ','Faith receives Christ’s righteousness; works do not purchase it','Luther locates justification in the promise received by faith, rather than in a price paid through deeds.',F,'Attributed paraphrase — Luther, Concerning Christian Liberty (1520), discussion of the Word and faith; public-domain English text.')
item('neighbour-in-need','The neighbour still needs food and practical help','Removing a price for salvation does not remove another person’s bodily need.',F,'Editorial case for the service argument in Luther, Concerning Christian Liberty (1520).')
item('christ-servant','Christ freely gives himself for another’s good','Luther uses Christ’s self-giving as a pattern for service that does not seek a return.',F,'Attributed paraphrase — Luther, Concerning Christian Liberty (1520), Christ’s example and service to the neighbour.')
item('freedom-from-merit','The neighbour’s need survives freedom from earning salvation','A gift received without a price leaves room for work whose beneficiary is someone else.',F,'Reconstructed partial argument — Luther, Concerning Christian Liberty (1520).')
item('received-and-given','Christ’s gift makes freely given service a fitting response','Receiving a gift and imitating its giver need not turn the response into payment.',F,'Reconstructed partial argument — Luther, Concerning Christian Liberty (1520).')
item('service-pattern','Christ’s self-giving points toward this neighbour’s need','The example supplies a direction for action: another person’s good rather than one’s own reward.',F,'Reconstructed application — Luther, Concerning Christian Liberty (1520).')
item('free-service','“Free lord of all”; “dutiful servant of all”','Free with respect to purchasing righteousness, serving with respect to the neighbour’s good: the two claims concern different relations.',F+['liberty'],'Two short quotations and reconstructed distinction — Luther, On Christian Liberty (1520), Lambert/Grimm translation, Hanover §3; argument checked against the public-domain text.')
item('receiving-not-earning','Helping us act and justifying us ask different questions','Augustine’s earlier argument explains inward assistance in willing and acting. Luther’s present claim identifies the basis on which a person is righteous. Both invoke grace; they are not interchangeable answers.',F+['aug-grace'],'Editorial comparison — Augustine, On the Grace of Christ I.3, 6–7; Luther, Concerning Christian Liberty (1520).')
item('works-after-faith','The 1517 food priority gains a 1520 reason: serve, not buy','The earlier choice to meet necessities can now be understood as helping people after a gift, rather than purchasing righteousness through generosity.',F+['theses'],'Editorial cross-date application — Luther, Ninety-Five Theses 43–46 (1517) and Concerning Christian Liberty (1520). Not a claim that the full 1520 argument was already stated in 1517.')
item('command-and-promise','Lifelong repentance is not a price for gifted righteousness','The continuing life demanded in 1517 need not be construed as instalments paid for the justification described in 1520.',F+['theses'],'Editorial comparison across Luther’s 1517 and 1520 texts; distinguishes the demand for a changed life from justification’s basis.')
item('body-discipline','Fasting and labour train the body and help other people','Luther retains bodily discipline and useful work, while denying that they purchase justification.',F,'Attributed paraphrase — Luther, Concerning Christian Liberty (1520), bodily discipline and neighbour service.')
item('body-not-discarded','Luther keeps bodily work, but not as justification’s price','A purpose for discipline and labour remains after their proposed purchasing role has been denied.',F,'Reconstructed distinction — Luther, Concerning Christian Liberty (1520). This is not an inference of two creators or a claim of Cathar influence.')
reasons={'retain':'This established argument remains available for a further substantive comparison.','incorporate':'This premise joins the manuscript as a preserved reference, not a discarded belief.','transform':'This partial argument expands; its cited premises remain preserved.'}
def rec(id,a,b,out,roles=('incorporate','incorporate')):
 # Event-local preservation keeps needed outside copies without losing manuscript references.
 roles=tuple('incorporate' if role=='retain' else role for role in roles)
 return {'id':id,'a':a,'b':b,'out':out,'sources':items[out]['sources'],'lifecycle':{k:{'role':v,'reason':reasons[v]} for k,v in zip((a,b),roles)},'preserve':[a,b]}
# The semantic sets below also document what each partial means. Tests use an independent oracle.
unions=[]
def tri(prefix,leaves,partials,out):
 sets={x:{x} for x in leaves};sets.update({p:set(pair) for p,pair in partials.items()});rs=[]
 for a,b in itertools.combinations(sets,2):
  u=sets[a]|sets[b]
  if u==sets[a] or u==sets[b]:continue
  result=out if len(u)==3 else next(p for p,ps in partials.items() if set(ps)==u)
  roles=('transform' if a in partials else 'incorporate','incorporate')
  rs.append(rec(prefix+'-'+a+'--'+b,a,b,result,roles))
 unions.append({'id':prefix,'leaves':leaves,'partials':partials,'out':out})
 return rs
